WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.960
All right, guys, so in this video, we're going to talk about balanced price range.

00:05.960 --> 00:10.000
Basically, it is another entry pattern that we use with Fair Value Gap.

00:10.640 --> 00:14.280
And what it does is it makes the Fair Value Gap even stronger.

00:14.320 --> 00:17.160
So let's assume that you have a Fair Value Gap here.

00:17.680 --> 00:24.040
And if we have BPR here as well, there are much more chances that that Fair Value Gap is

00:24.040 --> 00:27.080
going to hold the trade and it's going to go into your direction.

00:27.600 --> 00:34.560
Now, it's not necessary to always look for BPR, but, you know, it could be a nice

00:34.560 --> 00:38.120
confluence and it is also a nice entry pattern that you can use.

00:38.720 --> 00:40.240
So what is a BPR?

00:40.600 --> 00:46.160
So let's assume that you have a push, a move to the down, right?

00:46.640 --> 00:47.240
Boom.

00:48.240 --> 00:50.440
So let's assume there was some liquidity sweep right here.

00:50.920 --> 00:53.720
And after that, you have a market structure shift right here.

00:54.720 --> 00:58.280
Now, you look at the extreme Fair Value Gap, which is right here, right?

00:58.880 --> 01:00.800
And look at the left.

01:01.600 --> 01:05.600
So there is Fair Value Gap here as well, right?

01:05.960 --> 01:10.160
So you are looking at this Fair Value Gap, but if you look at to the left, the move

01:10.160 --> 01:14.000
that took out liquidity, now it necessarily don't have to be the move that takes out

01:14.000 --> 01:14.560
liquidity.

01:14.560 --> 01:16.400
I'm just, I'm just showing it as an example.

01:16.800 --> 01:20.640
But when you have a move and then you have a market structure shift, look at the last

01:20.920 --> 01:24.360
move before market structure shift and look at here.

01:25.080 --> 01:32.560
If there is Fair Value Gap here and there is Fair Value Gap in the new move that

01:32.640 --> 01:36.960
broke the market structure and the old Fair Value Gap and the new Fair Value

01:36.960 --> 01:39.360
Gap is overlapping with each other.

01:39.920 --> 01:45.760
If that is happening, then that area becomes BPR.

01:46.320 --> 01:46.680
Okay.

01:46.920 --> 01:49.440
And when price comes back into this area, it's going to push up.

01:50.440 --> 01:51.640
So let me explain this again.

01:53.880 --> 01:55.560
Do you see this move right here?

01:55.560 --> 01:57.840
Let me remove this to clear confusion, right?

01:58.640 --> 01:59.560
You have a move up.

02:00.000 --> 02:06.280
So let's assume you have lower low, lower high, sorry, lower low, lower high, lower low.

02:06.840 --> 02:09.880
And this lower low gets broken with market structure shift.

02:10.560 --> 02:12.320
And this is extreme Fair Value Gap.

02:12.320 --> 02:15.640
Now, normally you would enter from this extreme Fair Value Gap right here.

02:16.200 --> 02:20.200
But look to the left of this extreme Fair Value Gap.

02:20.840 --> 02:21.680
That's right here.

02:23.080 --> 02:32.920
If there is Fair Value Gap here as well, you know, and price does not respect the old

02:32.920 --> 02:37.840
Fair Value Gap and it pushes through this level, breaking market structure or shifting

02:37.840 --> 02:43.840
the market structure and also leaving the Fair Value Gap that overlaps with the

02:43.880 --> 02:49.680
previous Fair Value Gap, then that level becomes balanced price range.

02:50.560 --> 02:52.760
I hope I explained it well.

02:53.360 --> 02:55.840
And what I'm going to show you examples is going to be clear.

02:56.720 --> 02:59.280
So let me show you example number one.

03:01.280 --> 03:07.560
So you see you have lower low, lower high, lower low, and then you have a market structure shift.

03:07.560 --> 03:14.520
Look at here.

03:15.440 --> 03:17.640
Do you see this Fair Value Gap right here?

03:18.480 --> 03:19.720
From here to here.

03:21.040 --> 03:25.200
What happened is price did not respect this old Fair Value Gap.

03:25.640 --> 03:30.200
And what happened is price went back up and did market structure shift.

03:30.360 --> 03:30.760
Okay.

03:31.360 --> 03:35.600
Now, look at this, this Fair Value Gap is way too big.

03:35.640 --> 03:36.040
Okay.

03:37.040 --> 03:38.000
It's just way too big.

03:38.000 --> 03:42.720
So if you take your entry from the here, your stop loss is going to be huge.

03:43.000 --> 03:53.640
But if you look at here, you have BPR and the new Fair Value Gap is overlapping with the old Fair Value Gap.

03:55.160 --> 04:02.560
So in this case, what you can do is you can take your entry from the BPR or old Fair Value Gap.

04:03.480 --> 04:03.880
Okay.

04:04.560 --> 04:08.840
So essentially, what is happening here is that the old Fair Value Gap,

04:09.560 --> 04:17.600
instead of acting as a resistance level, when it is broken, it is not reacting as a support level,

04:17.680 --> 04:19.080
the old Fair Value Gap.

04:19.360 --> 04:25.080
And also, if there is new Fair Value Gap that is overlapping with the old Fair Value Gap,

04:25.600 --> 04:27.600
then that level becomes BPR.

04:27.840 --> 04:35.800
And trust me, that move is going to be big if your whole model was perfect.

04:37.640 --> 04:41.800
So you can see Fair Value BPR right here, it overlaps.

